import org.enforce.attributes.Attribute;
import org.enforce.attributes.Name;
import org.enforce.attributes.ClassName;

public class Main {

    public static boolean isValidTagName(String tagName) {
        return tagName.matches("^[a-zA-Z]+$");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String html = "<html><body src=\"image.jpg\" title=\"my page\"></body></html>";

        System.out.println(isValidTagName("img"));  // Output: true
        System.out.println(isValidTagName("<p>"));  // Output: false

        HtmlUtils.enforceAttributes(html);

        List<Attribute> enforcedAttributes = HtmlUtils.getEnforcedAttributes("img");

        for (Attribute attribute : enforcedAttributes) {
            System.out.println(attribute.getName() + ": " + attribute.getValue());
        }
    }
}
```

This Java code snippet demonstrates how to use the `HtmlUtils` class to enforce HTML attributes in a given HTML string.

In the `main` method, we create an example HTML string with two tags: "img" and "<p>". We then call the `enforceAttributes` method to validate and modify these tags according to their corresponding enforceable attributes.

We also retrieve the enforced attributes for the "img" tag using the `getEnforcedAttributes` method. This returns a list of all enforced attributes found in the HTML string, which we iterate over in the example usage section.

The `isValidTagName` method checks if a given tag name is valid by ensuring it only contains letters (both uppercase and lowercase). The `HtmlUtils.enforceAttributes` method uses this validation process to identify enforceable attributes in the HTML string.

Finally, we print out the names and values of all enforced attributes for the "img" tag.
